# ActiveSupport core_ext — community-maintained RBS bundle.
#
# Scope: the most-frequently-flagged ActiveSupport extension methods
# from a four-project Rails survey (Redmine, Discourse, Mastodon,
# GitLab FOSS — see `docs/notes/20260515-real-world-rails-survey.md`).
# Roughly the top ~40 selectors by `call.undefined-method` frequency,
# which together account for the bulk of the Rails-extension long
# tail.
#
# This file is opt-in: enable by adding the gem's `sig/` directory
# to `.rigor.yml`'s `signature_paths:`:
#
#     # .rigor.yml
#     signature_paths:
#       - sig
#       - path/to/rigor-activesupport-core-ext/sig
#
# Not exhaustive. ActiveSupport ships many more extensions across
# Hash / Array / String / Object / Date / Integer / Module than this
# file covers; PRs welcome to extend. `Time` is the ONE exception and
# is deliberately complete — it is a CORE class, so RBS knows it fully
# and it is CLOSED, which makes an omission on it a false positive
# rather than a missed precision opportunity (#658). `Date` and
# `DateTime` are closed for the same reason and are NOT complete yet.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Purity (ADR-103 WD10; docs/type-specification/effect-labels.md; issue #388)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `%a{pure}` below is audited one by one against the vendored ActiveSupport
# source (8.1.3.1), not assumed from the method name. Two recurring
# judgment calls, decided once here rather than repeated per method:
#
# - **Inflection rules are treated as pure.** `camelize` / `underscore` /
#   `pluralize` / `singularize` / `humanize` / `titleize` / `tableize` /
#   `classify` / `dasherize` / `demodulize` / `deconstantize` /
#   `foreign_key` / `upcase_first` all read `ActiveSupport::Inflector`'s
#   per-locale rule table (`inflections(locale)`) — process-global, but
#   configured once at boot and never touched by request-handling code.
#   Rigor's own ADR-90 (the standalone inflector) already treats
#   inflections as pure functions of the rule set; this file inherits
#   that assumption rather than re-litigating it. `parameterize` is the
#   one exception in this family: it calls `I18n.transliterate`, which
#   reads `I18n.locale` when no explicit `locale:` is given — a stronger,
#   request-scoped global read the ADR-90 assumption does not cover, so
#   it is skipped below.
# - **A method that dispatches to a name on its OWN receiver's declared
#   type is fine; a method that dispatches to a name looked up on an
#   ARGUMENT or ELEMENT of unknown type is not**, because the override is
#   invisible to this audit (the `try` problem). `in?` / `presence_in`
#   are the one deliberate exception: they call `#include?` / `#cover?`
#   on the argument, which is near-universally a pure predicate, and the
#   design note calls them out by name. `to_param` / `to_query` (Hash) and
#   `to_fs(:db)` (Array, which calls `#id` on each element) are the
#   converse case and are left unannotated.
#
# The clock, `Time.zone`, `ActiveSupport::Notifications` and
# `ActiveSupport::CurrentAttributes` are covered by this plugin's
# `effect_attributions:` manifest (`lib/rigor/plugin/activesupport_core_ext/effects.rb`,
# issue #387) rather than by an RBS annotation here — see that file for
# the impure half of this bundle. `DateTime#in_time_zone` was a gap in
# that sweep (it reads `Time.zone` via its default argument) and is
# closed there in this same change.
#
# Five `Date` singleton methods this file declares — `self.end_of_week`,
# `self.beginning_of_month`, `self.end_of_month`, `self.beginning_of_year`,
# `self.end_of_year` — do not exist in ActiveSupport 8.1
# (`Date.beginning_of_month` etc. are never defined; only the instance
# methods are). A sixth, `self.beginning_of_week`, DOES exist but with the
# wrong return type here (real `Date.beginning_of_week` is the week-start
# config getter, returning a `Symbol`, not a `Date`) — and reads
# fiber-local execution state either way. None of the six can be soundly
# annotated `%a{pure}`, so they are left exactly as they were; this
# paragraph is the flag for whoever fixes the six next (out of scope for
# the #388 purity sweep).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Object — universal predicates and helpers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

class Object
  # @return [bool]
  %a{pure}
  def blank?: () -> bool

  # @return [bool]
  %a{pure}
  def present?: () -> bool

  # @return [self?]
  %a{pure}
  def presence: () -> self?

  # `active_support/core_ext/object/json` monkey-patches
  # `as_json` onto Object (default returns the object's
  # `instance_values` hash) plus per-class overrides on
  # NilClass / TrueClass / FalseClass / Numeric / String /
  # Symbol / Time / Date / Array / Hash / Range / Regexp /
  # Struct / Enumerable. Most call-site code uses the
  # universal `obj.as_json` shape so a single Object row
  # restores type coverage at the Mastodon-derived
  # `as_json` call sites (`fan_out_on_write_service.rb`,
  # `push_update_worker.rb`, `media_component_helper.rb`).
  # `options` is the standard `only:` / `except:` / `methods:`
  # / `include:` hash — left as untyped because the surface
  # is large and per-call-site precision is rarely useful.
  #
  # NOT `%a{pure}`: `respond_to?(:to_hash) ? to_hash.as_json(options) :
  # instance_values.as_json(options)` dispatches to `to_hash` on the
  # receiver's own (unknown-at-this-declaration) class, exactly the way
  # `try` dispatches to an argument-named method — a subclass override is
  # invisible to this audit, and `Object` is as broad a receiver as RBS has.
  def as_json: (?untyped options) -> untyped

  # `try` swallows NoMethodError; declared return is `untyped`
  # because the called method's signature is not known to RBS
  # at this call site.
  #
  # NOT `%a{pure}`: `try` is pure only when the method it dispatches to is,
  # and that is a fact about the *call site*, not about `try` itself.
  def try: (Symbol | String) -> untyped
         | (Symbol | String, *untyped) -> untyped
         | (Symbol | String, *untyped) { (?) -> untyped } -> untyped

  # NOT `%a{pure}`, same reasoning as `try`.
  def try!: (Symbol | String) -> untyped
          | (Symbol | String, *untyped) -> untyped
          | (Symbol | String, *untyped) { (?) -> untyped } -> untyped

  # `acts_like?(:string)` / `acts_like?(:date)` / `acts_like?(:time)`
  # is ActiveSupport's "duck-typing helper" predicate: `respond_to?
  # :"acts_like_#{duck}?"`. Deterministic given the receiver's class.
  %a{pure}
  def acts_like?: (Symbol | String) -> bool

  # `core_ext/object/inclusion` — `x.in?([a, b])` / `x.in?(1..10)`. Dispatches
  # to `#include?` / `Range#cover?` on the ARGUMENT, the one deliberate
  # exception to the try-shaped-dispatch rule above (design note § 11.2).
  %a{pure}
  def in?: (untyped) -> bool
end

# `nil.blank?` / `nil.present?` / `nil.try` are the most frequent
# call sites the survey turned up.
class NilClass
  %a{pure}
  def blank?: () -> true
  %a{pure}
  def present?: () -> false
  %a{pure}
  def presence: () -> nil

  # Unlike `Object#try`, `NilClass#try` is a hardcoded override that
  # returns `nil` unconditionally — it never sends the message, so
  # unlike `Object#try` above it never dispatches at all.
  %a{pure}
  def try: (*untyped) -> nil
  %a{pure}
  def try!: (*untyped) -> nil
  %a{pure}
  def duplicable?: () -> true
  def instance_values: () -> Hash[String, untyped]
end

class TrueClass
  %a{pure}
  def blank?: () -> false
  %a{pure}
  def present?: () -> true
end

class FalseClass
  %a{pure}
  def blank?: () -> true
  %a{pure}
  def present?: () -> false
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# String — ActiveSupport `core_ext/string/*`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

class String
  # `core_ext/string/inflections` — the ADR-90 inflection-rule-table
  # assumption at the top of this file applies to the whole group below.
  %a{pure}
  def underscore: () -> String
  %a{pure}
  def camelize: (?Symbol upper_case_first) -> String
  %a{pure}
  def camelcase: (?Symbol upper_case_first) -> String
  %a{pure}
  def classify: () -> String
  def constantize: () -> untyped # NOT %a{pure}: resolves a constant, may trigger autoload.
  def safe_constantize: () -> untyped # NOT %a{pure}: same as constantize.
  %a{pure}
  def demodulize: () -> String
  %a{pure}
  def deconstantize: () -> String
  %a{pure}
  def titleize: () -> String
  %a{pure}
  def titlecase: () -> String
  %a{pure}
  def dasherize: () -> String
  %a{pure}
  def upcase_first: () -> String
  # NOT %a{pure}: transliterates through `I18n.transliterate`, which reads
  # `I18n.locale` when `locale:` is left at its `nil` default — a request-
  # scoped global read the inflection-table assumption does not cover.
  def parameterize: (?separator: String, ?preserve_case: bool, ?locale: Symbol?) -> String
  %a{pure}
  def tableize: () -> String
  %a{pure}
  def foreign_key: (?bool separate_class_name_and_id_with_underscore) -> String
  %a{pure}
  def pluralize: (?Integer count, ?Symbol locale) -> String
  %a{pure}
  def singularize: (?Symbol locale) -> String
  %a{pure}
  def humanize: (?capitalize: bool, ?keep_id_suffix: bool) -> String

  # `core_ext/string/filters`
  %a{pure}
  def squish: () -> String
  def squish!: () -> String # NOT %a{pure}: bang, mutates the receiver in place.
  %a{pure}
  def truncate: (Integer truncate_at, ?omission: String, ?separator: String | Regexp | nil) -> String
  %a{pure}
  def truncate_bytes: (Integer truncate_at, ?omission: String) -> String
  %a{pure}
  def truncate_words: (Integer words_count, ?omission: String, ?separator: String | Regexp | nil) -> String
  # `remove` / `remove!` delete every occurrence of the given patterns.
  %a{pure}
  def remove: (*Regexp | String patterns) -> String
  def remove!: (*Regexp | String patterns) -> String # NOT %a{pure}: bang, mutates in place.

  # `core_ext/string/output_safety` — `html_safe` returns an
  # `ActiveSupport::SafeBuffer` (a String subclass). The closest
  # RBS-canonical carrier is plain `String`; precision lost is the
  # `html_safe?` predicate value. Neither mutates the receiver; `html_safe?`
  # on a plain `String` receiver is a hardcoded `false`.
  %a{pure}
  def html_safe: () -> String
  %a{pure}
  def html_safe?: () -> bool

  # `core_ext/string/indent`
  %a{pure}
  def indent: (Integer amount, ?String indent_string, ?bool indent_empty_lines) -> String
  def indent!: (Integer amount, ?String indent_string, ?bool indent_empty_lines) -> String? # NOT %a{pure}: bang, mutates in place.

  # `core_ext/string/starts_ends_with` — also covered by core
  # `start_with?` / `end_with?`; these are the ActiveSupport aliases.
  %a{pure}
  def starts_with?: (*String prefixes) -> bool
  %a{pure}
  def ends_with?: (*String suffixes) -> bool

  # `core_ext/string/conversions` — `to_time` / `to_date` /
  # `to_datetime` return Time / Date / DateTime. NONE are `%a{pure}`:
  # all three default missing date/time components from `Time.now` /
  # the current date when the string under-specifies them (verified
  # against `activesupport/lib/active_support/core_ext/string/conversions.rb`)
  # — `nondet.time`, not a pure function of the string alone.
  def to_time: (?Symbol form) -> Time?
  def to_date: () -> Date?
  def to_datetime: () -> DateTime
  # `to_hours` does not exist in vendored ActiveSupport 8.1.3.1 — no
  # `String#to_hours` anywhere in `core_ext/string/`. Left unannotated
  # because there is no implementation here to audit; flagged for a
  # follow-up to confirm and either implement or remove the declaration.
  def to_hours: () -> Float

  # `core_ext/string/access`
  %a{pure}
  def at: (Integer | Range[Integer] | Regexp position) -> String?
  %a{pure}
  def from: (Integer position) -> String
  %a{pure}
  def to: (Integer position) -> String
  %a{pure}
  def first: (?Integer limit) -> String
  %a{pure}
  def last: (?Integer limit) -> String

  # `core_ext/string/strip` — leading-blank strip with shared margin.
  %a{pure}
  def strip_heredoc: () -> String

  # `core_ext/string/multibyte`
  %a{pure}
  def mb_chars: () -> untyped

  # `core_ext/string/inquiry`
  %a{pure}
  def inquiry: () -> untyped # ActiveSupport::StringInquirer
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Integer — `core_ext/integer/*`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

class Integer
  # `core_ext/numeric/time` — Duration multipliers. Each just wraps `self`
  # in an `ActiveSupport::Duration` value object (`Duration.seconds(self)`,
  # …); the clock is read only later, by calling `.ago` / `.since` / etc.
  # on the resulting Duration — attributed on `ActiveSupport::Duration` in
  # `lib/rigor/plugin/activesupport_core_ext/effects.rb`, not here.
  #
  # The `untyped` return is DELIBERATE and stays for now (#534). The real
  # answer is `ActiveSupport::Duration`, and the plugin supplies it from
  # `DURATION_MULTIPLIERS`, whose contribution tier sits above RBS
  # dispatch. `ActiveSupport::Duration` IS now declared in this bundle
  # (further down, before `Time`), protected by the manifest's
  # `open_receivers:` — so naming it here too is no longer blocked the way
  # it was when #534 landed. Issue #660 tracks moving this return type
  # into RBS to match; until then, what this declaration is for is
  # making `1.day` RESOLVE, and the type itself still comes from the plugin.
  %a{pure}
  def second: () -> untyped # ActiveSupport::Duration
  %a{pure}
  def seconds: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def minute: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def minutes: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def hour: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def hours: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def day: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def days: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def week: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def weeks: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def fortnight: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def fortnights: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def month: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def months: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def year: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def years: () -> untyped

  # `core_ext/numeric/bytes` — plain arithmetic on `self`.
  %a{pure}
  def byte: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def bytes: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def kilobyte: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def kilobytes: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def megabyte: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def megabytes: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def gigabyte: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def gigabytes: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def terabyte: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def terabytes: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def petabyte: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def petabytes: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def exabyte: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def exabytes: () -> Integer

  # `core_ext/integer/multiple`
  %a{pure}
  def multiple_of?: (Integer) -> bool

  # `core_ext/integer/inflections` — `ActiveSupport::Inflector.ordinal(ize)`,
  # the same inflection-rule-table assumption as the String group above.
  %a{pure}
  def ordinal: () -> String
  %a{pure}
  def ordinalize: () -> String
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Float — same Duration / Bytes pattern as Integer
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

class Float
  # Same reasoning as the `Integer` block above: value-object construction
  # and arithmetic only.
  %a{pure}
  def second: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def seconds: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def minute: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def minutes: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def hour: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def hours: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def day: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def days: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def week: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def weeks: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def month: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def months: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def year: () -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def years: () -> untyped

  %a{pure}
  def byte: () -> Float
  %a{pure}
  def bytes: () -> Float
  %a{pure}
  def kilobyte: () -> Float
  %a{pure}
  def kilobytes: () -> Float
  %a{pure}
  def megabyte: () -> Float
  %a{pure}
  def megabytes: () -> Float
  %a{pure}
  def gigabyte: () -> Float
  %a{pure}
  def gigabytes: () -> Float
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# ActiveSupport::Duration — issue #632
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

# The multiplier comment on `Integer#seconds` above explains why naming this class in RBS is normally the
# wrong move: an incomplete signature on a class whose real surface forwards to `method_missing` turns
# every omitted member into a false `call.undefined-method`. This declaration is the one place that trade
# is accepted anyway, because the manifest lists `ActiveSupport::Duration` under `open_receivers:` (ADR-26)
# — the same mechanism `rigor-activerecord` uses for `ActiveRecord::Relation` — so the rule never fires
# against a Duration receiver regardless of what this class does or doesn't declare. What follows is the
# reader surface audited against ActiveSupport 8.1.3.1's `lib/active_support/duration.rb`; every method not
# listed here (the arithmetic operators, `==`/`eql?`/`hash`/`<=>`, `to_s`/`inspect`, …) stays undeclared on
# purpose — some are already covered by the separate `dynamic_return` rule for `+`/`-`/`*` below, the rest
# are simply outside this issue's scope and remain silently `Dynamic` rather than a guess.
module ActiveSupport
  class Duration
    # `ago`/`until`/`before`/`since`/`from_now`/`after` are DELIBERATELY NOT declared here, reversing an
    # earlier version of this file (issue #659, blocked on #658). They default to `time = ::Time.current`,
    # so typing the zero-arg form needs the Rails `Time` INSTANCE surface (`to_fs`, `in_time_zone`, `past?`,
    # `future?`, `today?`, `formatted_offset`, `at_beginning_of_hour`, `middle_of_day`, and the rest of
    # `DateAndTime::Calculations` / `Zones` / `Conversions`) to already be closed and complete — `Time` is a
    # CORE Ruby class, RBS-known from stdlib, and this bundle's own `Time` block above never declared that
    # instance surface (#658 — already a live FP on master with the plugin loaded, independent of this
    # class: `Time.current.to_fs(:db)` fires today). Answering `ago` with `Time` therefore does not leave
    # the receiver silently `Dynamic` the way naming `ActiveSupport::Duration` does for its OWN undeclared
    # members (open_receivers only protects a receiver of the class it names) — it makes
    # `1.hour.ago.to_fs(:db)`, ordinary Rails, fire a false `call.undefined-method` for `to_fs` on `Time`,
    # and the same for every other Rails `Time` extension nothing here declares: 9 false positives measured
    # on one fixture, `to_fs` alone at ~92 corpus sites. #659 records the answer for when #658 lands so
    # nobody re-derives it: the return type is plain `Time`, not a `Time | ActiveSupport::TimeWithZone`
    # union — Rails overrides `TimeWithZone#is_a?` to answer true for `::Time`, so `Time` is the class Rails
    # ITSELF tells callers a `TimeWithZone` is, and the union was measured and degrades every downstream
    # chain to `Dynamic[top]`, equivalent to leaving `ago` undeclared. Do not re-add these methods until
    # #658 lands the `Time` surface first.

    # `to_i` returns `@value.to_i` — always Integer, whether `@value` itself is Integer (`1.day`) or Float
    # (`1.5.days`). `in_seconds` is a literal alias.
    %a{pure}
    def to_i: () -> Integer
    %a{pure}
    def in_seconds: () -> Integer

    # `to_f` is a real `ActiveSupport::Delegation.generate` accessor (defined at class-load time, not
    # `method_missing`) — `@value.to_f` is always Float.
    %a{pure}
    def to_f: () -> Float

    # `in_minutes` / `in_hours` / `in_days` / `in_weeks` / `in_months` / `in_years` are each
    # `in_seconds / SECONDS_PER_*.to_f` — Integer divided by Float is always Float.
    %a{pure}
    def in_minutes: () -> Float
    %a{pure}
    def in_hours: () -> Float
    %a{pure}
    def in_days: () -> Float
    %a{pure}
    def in_weeks: () -> Float
    %a{pure}
    def in_months: () -> Float
    %a{pure}
    def in_years: () -> Float

    # `iso8601(precision: nil)` delegates to `ISO8601Serializer#serialize`, which always returns a String.
    %a{pure}
    def iso8601: (?precision: Integer?) -> String

    # `parts` returns a `dup` of the internal parts hash (`{years:, months:, weeks:, days:, hours:,
    # minutes:, seconds:}`), Integer-valued for a duration built from an Integer multiplier but real
    # runtime Float for a fractional one (`1.5.seconds.parts == {seconds: 1.5}`) — `Hash[Symbol, Integer]`
    # was reviewed and rejected: a `Float`-valued `:seconds` entry read against that type would answer
    # `Integer`, and `parts[:seconds].nan?` on it would fire a false `call.undefined-method`, silent on
    # master where `parts` was undeclared. `Integer | Float` costs nothing on the common `parts[:days] * 2`
    # chain (both arms support `*`) and stays honest on the fractional one.
    %a{pure}
    def parts: () -> Hash[Symbol, Integer | Float]
  end
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Time — singleton + instance extensions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

class Time
  # NOT %a{pure}: already attributed nondet.time/global.read/global.write
  # by `lib/rigor/plugin/activesupport_core_ext/effects.rb` (issue #387).
  # Duplicating an RBS envelope here risks the two disagreeing.
  def self.current: () -> Time
  def self.zone: () -> untyped # ActiveSupport::TimeZone | nil
  def self.zone=: (String | Symbol | untyped) -> untyped

  # Issue #658, `core_ext/time/zones.rb` + `core_ext/time/calculations.rb`. The rest of the
  # singleton surface those two files add, none of it typed by rbs.
  #
  # `zone_default` is the `attr_accessor` behind `Time.zone`'s fallback; `find_zone!` raises on a
  # bad zone and `find_zone` rescues to `nil`; `use_zone` swaps the zone around a block and returns
  # the block's value. All four answer or accept an `ActiveSupport::TimeZone`, which this bundle
  # does not declare, so they stay `untyped` rather than guessing a carrier.
  #
  # NONE of them are `%a{pure}`. `zone_default` / `zone_default=` / `use_zone` are process-state
  # reads and writes, recorded in the plugin manifest next to `Time.zone` / `Time.zone=`.
  # `days_in_month` / `days_in_year` default their `year` to `current.year`, so an omitted argument
  # reads the clock — recorded there too. `find_zone!` / `find_zone` read the memoised
  # `ActiveSupport::TimeZone` registry and are left unannotated rather than claimed either way.
  def self.zone_default: () -> untyped
  def self.zone_default=: (untyped) -> untyped
  def self.use_zone: (untyped time_zone) { () -> untyped } -> untyped
  def self.find_zone!: (untyped time_zone) -> untyped
  def self.find_zone: (untyped time_zone) -> untyped
  # `COMMON_YEAR_DAYS_IN_MONTH[month]` answers `nil` for a month outside 1..12, but the declared
  # return stays `Integer`: `Integer?` would make `Time.days_in_month(2, 2024) - 1` — the way every
  # real call site uses it — a nil-arithmetic diagnostic, which is the more expensive error of the
  # two on correct code.
  def self.days_in_month: (Integer month, ?Integer year) -> Integer
  def self.days_in_year: (?Integer year) -> Integer
  # `Time.rfc3339(str)` builds a `Time` from the parsed parts, raising `ArgumentError` when the
  # time or offset component is missing. Distinct from the `rfc3339` INSTANCE alias below.
  def self.rfc3339: (String str) -> Time

  # `core_ext/time/conversions` — nothing to declare. `Time.parse`, `Time.httpdate` and
  # `Time.iso8601` all come from stdlib `time`, which rbs types; ActiveSupport only re-opens them.
  # Declaring them here raised `RBS::DuplicatedMethodDefinitionError` and collapsed the whole `Time`
  # definition to `Dynamic[top]`.

  # `core_ext/time/calculations` — every method below is computed from the
  # RECEIVER's own fields (`change` / `advance` / `+ seconds`), verified
  # against `activesupport/lib/active_support/core_ext/time/calculations.rb`.
  # None of them consult `Time.zone`; that is what distinguishes an
  # instance method here from the `self.*` singletons above.
  %a{pure}
  def yesterday: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def tomorrow: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def beginning_of_day: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def end_of_day: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def beginning_of_hour: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def end_of_hour: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def beginning_of_minute: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def end_of_minute: () -> Time
  # NOT %a{pure}: `start_day` defaults to `Date.beginning_of_week`, a
  # fiber-local config getter (`ActiveSupport::IsolatedExecutionState[:beginning_of_week]`)
  # — a global read when the argument is omitted.
  def beginning_of_week: (?Symbol start_day) -> Time
  def end_of_week: (?Symbol start_day) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def beginning_of_month: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def end_of_month: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def beginning_of_year: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def end_of_year: () -> Time
  # `Time#ago` / `#since` / `#in` (receiver a `Time`, not a `Duration`):
  # `since(seconds) = self + seconds`, purely computed from the receiver.
  # Distinct from `ActiveSupport::Duration#ago` (receiver a Duration,
  # reads the clock), attributed separately in `effects.rb`.
  %a{pure}
  def ago: (Numeric seconds) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def since: (Numeric seconds) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def in: (Numeric seconds) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def change: (**untyped) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_beginning_of_day: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_end_of_day: () -> Time
  # NOT %a{pure}: same `Date.beginning_of_week` default-argument read as
  # `beginning_of_week` / `end_of_week` above. They are `alias`es of those two in
  # `DateAndTime::Calculations`, so they take the same optional `start_day` — declaring them
  # zero-arity made `time.at_beginning_of_week(:sunday)`, correct Rails, an arity diagnostic.
  def at_beginning_of_week: (?Symbol start_day) -> Time
  def at_end_of_week: (?Symbol start_day) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_midnight: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_noon: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def midday: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def midnight: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def noon: () -> Time
  # NOTE: `Time#utc?` is NOT declared here — it is Ruby core, already typed by rbs's `core/time.rbs`.
  %a{pure}
  def acts_like_time?: () -> true
  # `advance(days: 1, months: -2)` and `all_day` (a `beginning_of_day..end_of_day` Range).
  # Both computed from the receiver only (`advance` goes through `to_date.gregorian.advance` +
  # `change`, never `Time.zone`; `all_day` composes the two pure `beginning_of_day` / `end_of_day`
  # above) — unlike the same-named methods on `Date` below.
  %a{pure}
  def advance: (untyped options) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def all_day: () -> Range[Time]

  # -------------------------------------------------------------
  # Issue #658 — the rest of the Rails `Time` INSTANCE surface
  # -------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `Time` is a CORE class, fully RBS-known from `core/time.rbs`, and therefore CLOSED: a name this
  # file does not declare is reported `call.undefined-method`. So on a closed class an OMISSION is a
  # false positive on correct Rails code just as much as a wrong return type is, and there is no
  # gradual middle — making `Time` an open receiver would suppress the genuine diagnostic
  # project-wide. Everything below was therefore audited name by name against the vendored
  # activesupport-8.1.3.1 sources rather than recalled: `core_ext/date_and_time/calculations.rb`
  # (the module `Time`, `Date` and `DateTime` all include), `core_ext/time/calculations.rb`,
  # `core_ext/time/conversions.rb`, `core_ext/date_and_time/zones.rb` and
  # `core_ext/date_and_time/compatibility.rb`.
  #
  # Every row was also checked against the assembled environment (`DEFAULT_LIBRARIES` + this `sig/`)
  # for a name rbs already declares, because a second full declaration of one raises
  # `RBS::DuplicatedMethodDefinitionError` and collapses the WHOLE class to `Dynamic[top]` — the
  # #437 failure the `Date#to_time` note below records. That is why `to_time`, `xmlschema`,
  # `localtime`, `getlocal`, `utc`, `getutc`, `utc?`, `utc_offset`, `usec`, `nsec`, `subsec`, `to_f`
  # and `to_i` are absent here even though ActiveSupport re-opens some of them: rbs types them already.

  # `core_ext/date_and_time/calculations.rb` — the shared module. Each of these is `advance(...)` /
  # `change(...)` on the receiver, so on a `Time` receiver each answers `Time`.
  #
  # NOT %a{pure}: the five predicates below compare against the CLOCK — `today?` / `tomorrow?` /
  # `yesterday?` read `::Date.current`, and `past?` / `future?` read `self.class.current`, which is
  # `Time.current` and therefore reads `Time.zone` as well.
  def today?: () -> bool
  def tomorrow?: () -> bool
  def next_day?: () -> bool
  def yesterday?: () -> bool
  def prev_day?: () -> bool
  def past?: () -> bool
  def future?: () -> bool
  # `WEEKEND_DAYS.include?(wday)` — the receiver's own field and a frozen constant.
  %a{pure}
  def on_weekend?: () -> bool
  %a{pure}
  def on_weekday?: () -> bool
  # NOT %a{pure}: `self < date_or_time` goes through ActiveSupport's own `<=>` override
  # (`compare_with_coercion`), which calls `comparable_time` / `to_time` on the ARGUMENT — a
  # dispatch on a value whose real class this audit cannot see, the `try` problem the file header
  # calls out. The parameter is `untyped` for the same reason: `Date`, `DateTime`,
  # `ActiveSupport::TimeWithZone` and `Time` are all accepted at runtime.
  def before?: (untyped date_or_time) -> bool
  def after?: (untyped date_or_time) -> bool
  # `days_ago(n)` … `years_since(n)` are all `advance(unit => ±n)`; the argument is `Numeric` and
  # not `Integer` because `advance` divmods a fractional `days:` / `weeks:` on purpose.
  %a{pure}
  def days_ago: (Numeric days) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def days_since: (Numeric days) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def weeks_ago: (Numeric weeks) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def weeks_since: (Numeric weeks) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def months_ago: (Numeric months) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def months_since: (Numeric months) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def years_ago: (Numeric years) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def years_since: (Numeric years) -> Time
  # The `at_`-prefixed spellings are `alias`es of the already-declared `beginning_of_*` /
  # `end_of_*`, and inherit their purity verdict one for one.
  %a{pure}
  def at_beginning_of_month: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_beginning_of_year: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_end_of_month: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_end_of_year: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def beginning_of_quarter: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_beginning_of_quarter: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def end_of_quarter: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_end_of_quarter: () -> Time
  # `(month / 3.0).ceil` — `Float#ceil` with no argument is Integer.
  %a{pure}
  def quarter: () -> Integer
  # NOT %a{pure}: `next_week` / `prev_week` / `last_week` / `days_to_week_start` / `all_week`
  # default their `start_day` to `Date.beginning_of_week`, the fiber-local config read
  # (`ActiveSupport::IsolatedExecutionState[:beginning_of_week]`) that already keeps
  # `beginning_of_week` / `end_of_week` above unannotated. `next_weekday` joins them because it
  # calls `next_week(:monday, same_time: true)`, whose body reaches `beginning_of_week` with NO
  # argument and `days_span`, both of which read the same config.
  def next_week: (?Symbol given_day_in_next_week, ?same_time: bool) -> Time
  def prev_week: (?Symbol start_day, ?same_time: bool) -> Time
  def last_week: (?Symbol start_day, ?same_time: bool) -> Time
  def next_weekday: () -> Time
  def days_to_week_start: (?Symbol start_day) -> Integer
  def all_week: (?Symbol start_day) -> Range[Time]
  # `prev_weekday` / `last_weekday` DO stay pure, and the asymmetry with `next_weekday` above is
  # real rather than an oversight: the body is `prev_day.on_weekend? ? copy_time_to(
  # beginning_of_week(:friday)) : prev_day`, and `:friday` is passed explicitly, so no default
  # argument ever reaches `Date.beginning_of_week`.
  %a{pure}
  def prev_weekday: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def last_weekday: () -> Time
  # `monday` / `sunday` likewise name their start day explicitly (`beginning_of_week(:monday)` /
  # `end_of_week(:monday)`), so unlike their zero-argument parents they read no config.
  %a{pure}
  def monday: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def sunday: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def next_quarter: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def prev_quarter: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def last_quarter: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def last_month: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def last_year: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def all_month: () -> Range[Time]
  %a{pure}
  def all_quarter: () -> Range[Time]
  %a{pure}
  def all_year: () -> Range[Time]
  %a{pure}
  def next_occurring: (Symbol day_of_week) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def prev_occurring: (Symbol day_of_week) -> Time

  # `core_ext/time/calculations.rb` — the `Time`-only half.
  #
  # `seconds_since_midnight` ends in `+ (usec / 1.0e+6)`, so it is Float even at exactly midnight;
  # `seconds_until_end_of_day` is `end_of_day.to_i - to_i`, Integer. `sec_fraction` is a plain alias
  # of `subsec`, and carries `subsec`'s own core type rather than a guessed `Rational` — CRuby
  # answers the Integer `0` for a whole-second receiver.
  %a{pure}
  def seconds_since_midnight: () -> Float
  %a{pure}
  def seconds_until_end_of_day: () -> Integer
  %a{pure}
  def sec_fraction: () -> (0 | Rational)
  # `middle_of_day` is `change(hour: 12)`, and `midday` / `noon` / `at_noon` (already declared
  # above) plus `at_midday` / `at_middle_of_day` are its aliases.
  %a{pure}
  def middle_of_day: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_midday: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_middle_of_day: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_beginning_of_hour: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_end_of_hour: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_beginning_of_minute: () -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def at_end_of_minute: () -> Time
  # `Time#prev_day` … `#next_year` are `Time`'s OWN redefinitions (`advance(days: -days)` and
  # friends), taking an optional count — not the zero-argument `Date` methods of the same name that
  # rbs's `stdlib/date` types. rbs does not declare them on `Time`, so these are additions.
  %a{pure}
  def prev_day: (?Numeric days) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def next_day: (?Numeric days) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def prev_month: (?Numeric months) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def next_month: (?Numeric months) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def prev_year: (?Numeric years) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def next_year: (?Numeric years) -> Time

  # `core_ext/time/conversions.rb`.
  #
  # NOT %a{pure}: `to_fs` / `to_formatted_s` look the format up in `Time::DATE_FORMATS`, a mutable
  # process-global hash that applications are documented to extend from an initializer, and a
  # custom entry may be an arbitrary lambda that is then CALLED. That is the same reason
  # `Array#to_fs` is unannotated above. Both always answer a String, whatever the formatter
  # returns: the lambda branch ends in `.to_s` and the fallback is `to_s`.
  def to_fs: (?Symbol format) -> String
  def to_formatted_s: (?Symbol format) -> String
  # `formatted_offset` is `utc? && alternate_utc_string || ActiveSupport::TimeZone
  # .seconds_to_utc_offset(utc_offset, colon)`, and `seconds_to_utc_offset` is a `format %` over
  # its two arguments — pure, and String in both branches. `alternate_utc_string` is `untyped`
  # because it is returned as-is when the receiver is UTC and callers pass whatever they want to
  # print there.
  %a{pure}
  def formatted_offset: (?bool colon, ?untyped alternate_utc_string) -> String
  # `rfc3339` is `alias_method :rfc3339, :xmlschema`, so it takes `xmlschema`'s optional
  # `fraction_digits`. rbs types `Time#xmlschema` but not `Time#rfc3339`, so this is an addition
  # rather than a collision.
  %a{pure}
  def rfc3339: (?Integer fraction_digits) -> String

  # `core_ext/date_and_time/zones.rb`. Answers an `ActiveSupport::TimeWithZone`, which nothing in
  # this bundle declares, so the return is `untyped` rather than a guess — the same answer
  # `DateTime#in_time_zone` gives below. The parameter is `untyped` and not `String | Symbol`
  # because `Time.find_zone!` also accepts an `ActiveSupport::TimeZone`, a `TZInfo::Timezone`, and
  # an offset `Duration` (`Time.find_zone! -5.hours` is the documented spelling).
  #
  # NOT %a{pure}: `in_time_zone(zone = ::Time.zone)` reads `Time.zone` through its own default
  # argument. The label is `global.read` alone and it is recorded in the plugin manifest
  # (`lib/rigor/plugin/activesupport_core_ext/effects.rb`), next to the identical `DateTime` row.
  def in_time_zone: (?untyped zone) -> untyped

  # `core_ext/date_and_time/compatibility.rb` — `mattr_accessor :utc_to_local_returns_utc_offset_times,
  # instance_writer: false` defines an instance READER on every class that includes the module.
  # Rarely called on an instance, but it is public and `Time` is closed, so the omission would be
  # the false positive. NOT %a{pure}: it reads process-global configuration.
  def utc_to_local_returns_utc_offset_times: () -> bool
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Date — `core_ext/date/*`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

class Date
  # NOT %a{pure}: `self.current` / `self.yesterday` / `self.tomorrow` are
  # already attributed nondet.time/global.read by `effects.rb` (#387).
  # The other five singletons declared here — `self.beginning_of_week`
  # through `self.end_of_year` — do not soundly exist; see the file-header
  # note. None get an annotation.
  def self.current: () -> Date
  def self.yesterday: () -> Date
  def self.tomorrow: () -> Date
  def self.beginning_of_week: () -> Date
  def self.end_of_week: () -> Date
  def self.beginning_of_month: () -> Date
  def self.end_of_month: () -> Date
  def self.beginning_of_year: () -> Date
  def self.end_of_year: () -> Date

  # `advance(days: -1)` / `advance(days: 1)` — pure y/m/d arithmetic on the
  # receiver (`core_ext/date_and_time/calculations.rb`), unlike the
  # `self.yesterday` / `self.tomorrow` singletons above.
  %a{pure}
  def yesterday: () -> Date
  %a{pure}
  def tomorrow: () -> Date
  # NOT %a{pure}: `start_day` defaults to `Date.beginning_of_week`, a
  # fiber-local config read — same reasoning as `Time#beginning_of_week`.
  def beginning_of_week: (?Symbol start_day) -> Date
  def end_of_week: (?Symbol start_day) -> Date
  %a{pure}
  def beginning_of_month: () -> Date
  %a{pure}
  def end_of_month: () -> Date
  %a{pure}
  def beginning_of_year: () -> Date
  %a{pure}
  def end_of_year: () -> Date
  # NOT %a{pure}: `Date#ago` / `#since` route through `in_time_zone`
  # (`in_time_zone.since(...)`), which reads `Time.zone` by default —
  # unlike `Time#ago` / `#since` above, which never leave the receiver.
  def ago: (Numeric seconds) -> Time
  def since: (Numeric seconds) -> Time
  %a{pure}
  def acts_like_date?: () -> true

  # `core_ext/date/calculations` — `Date#midnight` /
  # `Date#at_midnight` are aliases for `beginning_of_day`.
  # Rails' `beginning_of_day` on Date returns a Time at
  # midnight of that day (not a Date). Mastodon's
  # `Date.current.at_midnight` shape relies on this.
  #
  # NONE of the six below are `%a{pure}`: every one is `in_time_zone.xxx`
  # (`core_ext/date/zones.rb`, `DateAndTime::Zones#in_time_zone`), and
  # `in_time_zone(zone = ::Time.zone)` reads `Time.zone` when no explicit
  # zone is given. This is the one place `Date` and `Time` diverge on the
  # exact same method names — verify against source before trusting the
  # name alone.
  def beginning_of_day: () -> Time
  def midnight: () -> Time
  def at_midnight: () -> Time
  def at_beginning_of_day: () -> Time
  def end_of_day: () -> Time
  def at_end_of_day: () -> Time
  # `advance(days: 1)` is pure y/m/d arithmetic on the receiver, same as
  # `yesterday` / `tomorrow` above. `all_day` is NOT %a{pure}: it composes
  # `beginning_of_day..end_of_day`, and both of those read `Time.zone` on
  # `Date` (unlike on `Time`, where `all_day` is pure). `to_time(form)` is
  # `Time.public_send(form, year, month, day)` — pure, all three components
  # come from the receiver and none default from the clock (contrast
  # `String#to_time` above, which does).
  %a{pure}
  def advance: (untyped options) -> Date
  def all_day: () -> Range[Time]
  # NOTE: `Date#to_time` is an OVERLOAD CONTINUATION (`| ...`), not a plain
  # declaration — stdlib `date` already types `to_time: () -> Time`, and a
  # second full declaration of the same method from a second signature source
  # makes `RBS::DefinitionBuilder` raise `DuplicatedMethodDefinitionError`,
  # which collapses `Date` AND `DateTime` to `Dynamic[top]` for every project
  # that activates this plugin (#437 — the same failure the `DateTime#to_time`
  # note below records). The row cannot simply be DROPPED the way
  # `DateTime#to_time` was: ActiveSupport genuinely widens the arity, and
  # without the row `date.to_time(:utc)` — correct Rails code — would draw an
  # arity diagnostic. `| ...` appends this overload ahead of the stdlib one, so
  # both arities resolve and the `%a{pure}` envelope stays attached to the
  # ActiveSupport overload (which is the one a no-argument call selects).
  # `| ...` requires the base declaration to exist, which `Environment.for_project`
  # guarantees: it merges `DEFAULT_LIBRARIES` — including `date` — into every
  # environment that loads a plugin's `signature_paths`.
  %a{pure}
  def to_time: (?Symbol form) -> Time | ...
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Array — singleton wrapper + instance extensions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

class Array[unchecked out Elem]
  # `Array.wrap(x)` is the dominant Rails idiom: `nil → []`,
  # `Array x → x`, `else → [x]`.
  %a{pure}
  def self.wrap: (untyped) -> Array[untyped]

  # `core_ext/array/access`
  %a{pure}
  def from: (Integer position) -> Array[Elem]
  %a{pure}
  def to: (Integer position) -> Array[Elem]
  %a{pure}
  def second: () -> Elem?
  %a{pure}
  def third: () -> Elem?
  %a{pure}
  def fourth: () -> Elem?
  %a{pure}
  def fifth: () -> Elem?
  %a{pure}
  def forty_two: () -> Elem?

  # `core_ext/array/grouping`
  %a{pure}
  def in_groups_of: (Integer number, ?untyped fill_with) -> Array[Array[Elem]]
                  | (Integer number, ?untyped fill_with) { (Array[Elem]) -> void } -> Array[Elem]
  %a{pure}
  def in_groups: (Integer number, ?untyped fill_with) -> Array[Array[Elem]]
               | (Integer number, ?untyped fill_with) { (Array[Elem]) -> void } -> Array[Elem]
  %a{pure}
  def split: (?untyped value) -> Array[Array[Elem]]
           | () { (Elem) -> bool } -> Array[Array[Elem]]

  # `core_ext/array/conversions`. NEITHER `to_sentence` nor `to_fs` /
  # `to_formatted_s` is `%a{pure}`: `to_sentence` calls `I18n.translate`
  # for its connector words unless `locale: false` is passed explicitly
  # (a request-scoped global read); `to_fs(:db)` calls `#id` on every
  # element, a dispatch to an unknown-at-this-declaration method the same
  # way `Object#try` does. `to_xml` takes a caller-supplied `:builder` and
  # an optional block, both opaque callables — skipped for the same reason.
  def to_sentence: (?two_words_connector: String, ?last_word_connector: String, ?words_connector: String, ?locale: Symbol?) -> String
  def to_formatted_s: (?Symbol format) -> String
  def to_fs: (?Symbol format) -> String
  def to_xml: (**untyped) -> String

  # `core_ext/array/inquiry`
  %a{pure}
  def inquiry: () -> untyped # ActiveSupport::ArrayInquirer

  # `core_ext/array/extract`
  def extract!: () { (Elem) -> bool } -> Array[Elem] # NOT %a{pure}: bang, mutates in place.

  # `core_ext/object/blank` / `core_ext/enumerable` — both
  # ActiveSupport additions, shipped on Array specifically.
  %a{pure}
  def compact_blank: () -> Array[Elem]
  def compact_blank!: () -> self # NOT %a{pure}: bang (`delete_if`), mutates in place.
  %a{pure}
  def exclude?: (Elem) -> bool
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Enumerable — `core_ext/enumerable`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

module Enumerable[unchecked out Elem]
  %a{pure}
  def index_by: () { (Elem) -> untyped } -> Hash[untyped, Elem]
  %a{pure}
  def index_with: (?untyped default) { (Elem) -> untyped } -> Hash[Elem, untyped]
                | (untyped default) -> Hash[Elem, untyped]
  %a{pure}
  def exclude?: (Elem) -> bool
  %a{pure}
  def including: (*Elem) -> Array[Elem]
  %a{pure}
  def excluding: (*Elem) -> Array[Elem]
  %a{pure}
  def without: (*Elem) -> Array[Elem]
  %a{pure}
  def pluck: (Symbol | String) -> Array[untyped]
           | (*Symbol | String) -> Array[Array[untyped]]
  %a{pure}
  def pick: (Symbol | String) -> untyped
          | (*Symbol | String) -> Array[untyped]?
  %a{pure}
  def maximum: (Symbol | String) -> untyped
  %a{pure}
  def minimum: (Symbol | String) -> untyped
  # Raises `SoleItemExpectedError` rather than returning on 0-or-2+ items;
  # raising is not an effect label (AGENTS.md / design note), so this is
  # still pure.
  %a{pure}
  def sole: () -> Elem
  %a{pure}
  def compact_blank: () -> Array[Elem]
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Hash — `core_ext/hash/*`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

class Hash[unchecked out K, unchecked out V]
  # `core_ext/hash/keys` — bang variants mutate in place, so only the
  # non-bang half is `%a{pure}`. `assert_valid_keys` raises rather than
  # mutating (raising is not an effect label).
  %a{pure}
  def symbolize_keys: () -> Hash[Symbol, V]
  def symbolize_keys!: () -> self
  %a{pure}
  def deep_symbolize_keys: () -> Hash[untyped, untyped]
  def deep_symbolize_keys!: () -> self
  %a{pure}
  def stringify_keys: () -> Hash[String, V]
  def stringify_keys!: () -> self
  %a{pure}
  def deep_stringify_keys: () -> Hash[untyped, untyped]
  def deep_stringify_keys!: () -> self
  %a{pure}
  def assert_valid_keys: (*K | Array[K]) -> self
  %a{pure}
  def deep_transform_keys: () { (K) -> K } -> Hash[K, untyped]
  def deep_transform_keys!: () { (K) -> K } -> self
  %a{pure}
  def deep_transform_values: () { (V) -> untyped } -> Hash[K, untyped]
  def deep_transform_values!: () { (V) -> untyped } -> self

  # `core_ext/hash/deep_dup` — allocates a deep copy, never touches the
  # receiver.
  %a{pure}
  def deep_dup: () -> Hash[K, V]

  # `core_ext/hash/deep_merge` — the block receives `(key, this_val,
  # other_val)` and returns the merged value; neither `deep_merge` nor its
  # block mutates either hash (containment covers the block itself).
  %a{pure}
  def deep_merge: (Hash[K, V]) -> Hash[K, V]
                | (Hash[K, V]) { (K, V, V) -> V } -> Hash[K, V]
  def deep_merge!: (Hash[K, V]) -> self
                 | (Hash[K, V]) { (K, V, V) -> V } -> self

  # `core_ext/hash/except` — `Hash#except` is in core RBS as of
  # Ruby 3.0+; `except!` is ActiveSupport-only. ActiveSupport
  # also aliases `Hash#without` to `Hash#except`, used by
  # `Mastodon`-shaped `options.without('type').merge(...)` chains.
  def except!: (*K) -> self # NOT %a{pure}: bang, mutates via `delete`.
  %a{pure}
  def without: (*K) -> Hash[K, V]

  # `core_ext/hash/conversions` — NOT `%a{pure}`: `to_query` / `to_param`
  # recurse into `value.to_query` / `value.to_param` on every value, an
  # unknown-at-this-declaration dispatch the same way `Object#try` and
  # `Array#to_fs(:db)` are (a value's own `to_param` override is invisible
  # here); `to_xml` takes a caller-supplied `:builder` and an optional
  # block, both opaque callables.
  def to_query: (?String namespace) -> String
  def to_param: (?String namespace) -> String
  def to_xml: (**untyped) -> String

  # `core_ext/hash/indifferent_access` — wraps `self` in a new
  # `HashWithIndifferentAccess`; the design note § 11.2 names this row
  # `%a{pure}` explicitly.
  %a{pure}
  def with_indifferent_access: () -> untyped

  # `core_ext/hash/conversions`
  def self.from_xml: (String, ?Symbol disallowed_types) -> Hash[String, untyped]
  def self.from_trusted_xml: (String) -> Hash[String, untyped]

  # `core_ext/object/blank` / `core_ext/hash`
  %a{pure}
  def compact_blank: () -> Hash[K, V]
  def compact_blank!: () -> self # NOT %a{pure}: bang (`delete_if`), mutates in place.

  # `core_ext/hash/reverse_merge`
  %a{pure}
  def reverse_merge: (Hash[K, V]) -> Hash[K, V]
  def reverse_merge!: (Hash[K, V]) -> self # NOT %a{pure}: bang (`replace`), mutates in place.

  # `core_ext/hash/slice` — `Hash#except` is in core RBS (Ruby 3.0+);
  # `Hash#slice` is in core RBS (Ruby 2.5+); the bang variants are
  # ActiveSupport-only.
  def slice!: (*K) -> Hash[K, V] # NOT %a{pure}: bang (`replace`), mutates in place.
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# DateTime — `core_ext/date_time/*`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

class DateTime
  # `utc` is pure Rational arithmetic on the receiver's own offset
  # (`core_ext/date_time/calculations.rb`), no zone lookup.
  %a{pure}
  def utc: () -> Time
  # NOTE: `DateTime#to_time` is NOT declared here — stdlib `date` already types it.
  # NOT %a{pure}: `in_time_zone(zone = ::Time.zone)` reads `Time.zone`
  # when no explicit zone is passed (`core_ext/date_and_time/zones.rb`).
  # Not previously covered by #387's `effect_attributions:`; the plugin
  # manifest gains this row in the same change (`global.read`).
  def in_time_zone: (?String | Symbol zone) -> untyped
  # `yesterday` / `tomorrow` are inherited from `DateAndTime::Calculations`
  # via `Date` (`DateTime < Date`), resolved against `DateTime`'s own
  # `advance` — pure, same reasoning as `Date#yesterday` / `#tomorrow`
  # above and unlike `Date.self.yesterday` / `.tomorrow`.
  %a{pure}
  def yesterday: () -> DateTime
  %a{pure}
  def tomorrow: () -> DateTime
  # `ago` / `since` are `self + Rational(seconds, 86400)` — pure, and
  # (unlike `Date#ago` / `#since`) never route through `in_time_zone`.
  %a{pure}
  def ago: (Numeric seconds) -> DateTime
  %a{pure}
  def since: (Numeric seconds) -> DateTime
  %a{pure}
  def beginning_of_day: () -> DateTime
  %a{pure}
  def end_of_day: () -> DateTime
  %a{pure}
  def beginning_of_hour: () -> DateTime
  %a{pure}
  def end_of_hour: () -> DateTime
  %a{pure}
  def beginning_of_minute: () -> DateTime
  %a{pure}
  def end_of_minute: () -> DateTime
  %a{pure}
  def acts_like_time?: () -> true
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# String — `core_ext/string/exclude` (an ActiveSupport addition)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

# (Re-opens String to add `#exclude?`. Listed here rather than in the
# main String block above because it's a less-common method whose
# coverage was added after a second-round survey.)
class String
  %a{pure}
  def exclude?: (String) -> bool
end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# ERB::Util — ActionView extends it with `html_escape_once`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

# `ERB` is a CLASS in Ruby / RBS (not a module), so reopen it as a class —
# a `module ERB` wrapper collides with upstream `class ERB` once the `erb`
# stdlib is in scope. `ERB::Util` itself is a module.
class ERB
  module Util
    # `ERB::Util.html_escape_once(s)` escapes HTML without double-escaping
    # an already-escaped entity. ActionView adds it on top of stdlib ERB.
    # A deterministic regex-substitution transform of the argument; no
    # receiver, no global reads.
    %a{pure}
    def self.html_escape_once: (untyped) -> String
  end
end
